Instead of changing indegrees, this version uses an explicit stack and DFS colors. The stack stores pairs containing a node and whether its outgoing edges have already been explored.

A node is added after its neighbors, then the collected list is reversed. This also catches cycles: reaching a gray node means the current path has returned to an unfinished vertex.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> topoSort(int V, vector<int> adj[]){
        vector<int> color(V, 0);
        vector<int> finish;

        for (int start = 0; start < V; start++) {
            if (color[start] != 0) {
                continue;
            }

            stack<pair<int, bool>> work;
            work.push({start, false});
            color[start] = 1;

            while (!work.empty()) {
                auto [node, exiting] = work.top();
                work.pop();

                if (exiting) {
                    color[node] = 2;
                    finish.push_back(node);
                    continue;
                }

                work.push({node, true});
                for (int i = (int)adj[node].size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
                    int next = adj[node][i];
                    if (color[next] == 1) {
                        return {};
                    }
                    if (color[next] == 0) {
                        color[next] = 1;
                        work.push({next, false});
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        reverse(finish.begin(), finish.end());
        return finish;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)` because every vertex and edge is examined a constant number of times.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the colors, stack, and result. The input adjacency array is not changed. A graph with no edges simply returns all vertices in their scan order.

This is a simple version that repeatedly scans all vertices to find one with indegree zero. After choosing it, the code lowers the indegree of its outgoing neighbors.

The scan is slower than using a queue, but it avoids another ready-node data structure and is easy to trace. If no unused zero-indegree node can be found before all vertices are placed, the graph contains a cycle.

```javascript
class Solution {
    topoSort(V, adj) {
        const indegree = new Array(V).fill(0);
        for (let node = 0; node < V; node++) {
            for (const next of adj[node]) {
                indegree[next]++;
            }
        }

        const used = new Array(V).fill(false);
        const order = [];

        while (order.length < V) {
            let chosen = -1;
            for (let node = 0; node < V; node++) {
                if (!used[node] && indegree[node] === 0) {
                    chosen = node;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (chosen === -1) {
                return [];
            }

            used[chosen] = true;
            order.push(chosen);
            for (const next of adj[chosen]) {
                indegree[next]--;
            }
        }

        return order;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V^2 + E)`, since one full scan may be needed for each vertex.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the local arrays. An empty graph is handled naturally and returns an empty ordering.

A normal queue can return any valid order. Here I used a min heap so the smallest currently available vertex is selected first. This makes the result predictable, while it still follows the same indegree rule.

Dry run: for edges `0 -> 2` and `1 -> 2`, the heap starts with `0` and `1`. It removes `0`, then `1`, and only then does node `2` reach indegree zero.

```python
import heapq

class Solution:
    def topoSort(self, V, adj):
        indegree = [0] * V
        for node in range(V):
            for next_node in adj[node]:
                indegree[next_node] += 1

        available = []
        for node in range(V):
            if indegree[node] == 0:
                heapq.heappush(available, node)

        order = []
        while available:
            node = heapq.heappop(available)
            order.append(node)

            for next_node in adj[node]:
                indegree[next_node] -= 1
                if indegree[next_node] == 0:
                    heapq.heappush(available, next_node)

        return order if len(order) == V else []
```

Time complexity: `O((V + E) log V)` because each available vertex is handled by the heap.

Space complexity: `O(V)`, not counting the adjacency list. The input adjacency lists are only read, not modified.

I kept the usual Kahn idea here. First, count the incoming edges for every node. Nodes with indegree zero can be placed immediately. After removing one, its neighbors may become available.

The graph is not changed. Only the local indegree array is updated. For example, with edges `0 -> 1` and `0 -> 2`, node `0` starts in the queue. Removing it makes both `1` and `2` available.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> topoSort(int V, vector<int> adj[]){
        vector<int> indegree(V, 0);
        for (int node = 0; node < V; node++) {
            for (int next : adj[node]) {
                indegree[next]++;
            }
        }

        queue<int> ready;
        for (int node = 0; node < V; node++) {
            if (indegree[node] == 0) {
                ready.push(node);
            }
        }

        vector<int> order;
        while (!ready.empty()) {
            int node = ready.front();
            ready.pop();
            order.push_back(node);

            for (int next : adj[node]) {
                indegree[next]--;
                if (indegree[next] == 0) {
                    ready.push(next);
                }
            }
        }

        if ((int)order.size() != V) {
            return {};
        }
        return order;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.

Space complexity: `O(V)` for the indegree array, queue, and result. If the graph has a cycle, the result is empty because not all vertices can be processed.

This version uses depth first search instead of a queue. A node is added to the answer only after all nodes reachable from it have been visited, so the final list is reversed at the end.

There are three states for each node: not visited, currently visiting, and finished. Seeing a currently visiting node means there is a cycle, so an empty array is returned.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    private int[] state;
    private List<Integer> finishOrder;
    private boolean hasCycle;

    public int[] topoSort(int V, List<List<Integer>> adj) {
        state = new int[V];
        finishOrder = new ArrayList<>();
        hasCycle = false;

        for (int node = 0; node < V; node++) {
            if (state[node] == 0) {
                dfs(node, adj);
            }
        }

        if (hasCycle) {
            return new int[0];
        }

        Collections.reverse(finishOrder);
        int[] answer = new int[V];
        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {
            answer[i] = finishOrder.get(i);
        }
        return answer;
    }

    private void dfs(int node, List<List<Integer>> adj) {
        if (hasCycle) {
            return;
        }

        state[node] = 1;
        for (int next : adj.get(node)) {
            if (state[next] == 1) {
                hasCycle = true;
                return;
            }
            if (state[next] == 0) {
                dfs(next, adj);
            }
        }

        state[node] = 2;
        finishOrder.add(node);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(V + E)`.

Space complexity: `O(V)` apart from the input graph. The recursive call stack can also reach `O(V)`. A deep graph may therefore be less comfortable for recursion than an iterative method.

