class Solution{
public:
    vector<int> topoSort(int V, vector<int> adj[]){
        vector<int> ind(V,0);
        vector<int> ans;
        queue<int>q;

        for(int i =0;i<V;i++){
            for(int j =0;j<adj[i].size();j++){
                ind[adj[i][j]]++;
            }
        }
        for(int i =0;i<V;i++){
            if(ind[i]==0){
                q.push(i);
            }
        }

        while(!q.empty()){
            int a = q.front();
            q.pop();
            ans.push_back(a);
            for(int i : adj[a]){
                ind[i]--;
                if(ind[i] == 0){
                    q.push(i);
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(V + E)

Breaking it down:
- Computing in-degrees: you iterate over every node's adjacency list once → total work across all lists = O(E) (sum of all edges).
- Initial queue seeding: loop over all V nodes once → O(V).
- BFS traversal: each node is pushed and popped from the queue exactly once → O(V). For each popped node, you iterate its adjacency list → across all nodes, this totals O(E) (each edge is processed exactly once, when decrementing the neighbor's in-degree).

Total: O(V) + O(E) + O(V) + O(E) = O(V + E)

